# 5. HCI DESIGN REQUIREMENTS

This section directly addresses the Good HCI Design requirements of the project.

## 5.1 User-Friendly Interface

- Provide a clean and understandable interface.
- Avoid unnecessary visual clutter.
- Use recognizable controls and meaningful labels.
- Display the most important actions prominently.
- Keep related information together.
- Minimize unnecessary user effort.

## 5.2 Clear and Logical Navigation

- Provide a consistent header and navigation menu.
- Organize the main services into clearly labeled sections.
- Provide breadcrumbs on deeper pages where useful.
- Highlight the current navigation destination.
- Keep navigation consistent across related screens.
- Provide clear paths back to search results and dashboards.

## 5.3 Appropriate Layout

- Use a responsive grid for property cards.
- Use readable content widths for forms and detailed descriptions.
- Align related fields and controls consistently.
- Use appropriate whitespace to separate sections.
- Maintain a clear visual hierarchy.
- Avoid overcrowding pages with unrelated information.

## 5.4 Consistency

- Use the same button styles for the same types of actions.
- Maintain consistent card layouts and form controls.
- Use consistent labels for similar concepts.
- Maintain consistent navigation placement.
- Reuse colors, typography, spacing, and component patterns.
- Keep success, warning, and error feedback consistent.

## 5.5 Readability

- Use short headings and concise descriptions.
- Maintain comfortable line spacing.
- Use readable font sizes.
- Avoid excessively long paragraphs in interface content.
- Make prices, property locations, and key specifications easy to scan.
- Use tables or aligned cards when comparing properties.

## 5.6 Appropriate Use of Colors

Use a professional real-estate color palette:

- Primary: deep navy blue.
- Secondary: teal or turquoise.
- Accent: warm gold for selective highlights.
- Background: white and very light gray.
- Primary text: dark slate.
- Success: accessible green.
- Warning: accessible amber.
- Error: accessible red.

Colors must be used consistently. Text must maintain sufficient contrast against its background. Avoid excessive gradients, neon colors, and unnecessary decorative effects.

## 5.7 Typography

- Use Inter or a comparable readable sans-serif typeface.
- Use clear heading levels.
- Make page titles visually distinct from section headings.
- Use consistent font weights and sizes.
- Use comfortable line height for paragraphs.
- Ensure forms, prices, and property specifications remain legible on mobile devices.

## 5.8 Effective Buttons and Controls

- Use descriptive button labels such as "Search Properties," "Find My Match," "Generate Design," and "Contact Seller."
- Make primary actions visually prominent.
- Use secondary buttons for less important actions.
- Provide hover, focus, pressed, disabled, and loading states where applicable.
- Prevent accidental duplicate submissions during processing.
- Ensure buttons perform real prototype actions.

## 5.9 Clear Feedback

- Display success messages after saving a listing or updating a profile.
- Show validation messages beside relevant form fields.
- Display loading indicators during processing.
- Explain why an action failed and how to recover.
- Show clear confirmation before deleting listings.
- Display empty states when no results exist.

## 5.10 Error Prevention

- Validate required fields before submission.
- Enforce sensible minimum and maximum values.
- Check price ranges and prevent invalid ranges.
- Validate uploaded file types and sizes.
- Confirm destructive actions.
- Preserve entered form data when validation fails.
- Show connection-plan duration and cost before simulated checkout confirmation.

## 5.11 Accessibility

- Support keyboard navigation.
- Provide visible focus indicators.
- Use semantic headings and landmarks.
- Associate form labels with their controls.
- Provide text alternatives for meaningful images.
- Ensure sufficient contrast.
- Avoid relying solely on color to communicate status.
- Support responsive zoom and text resizing.

## 5.12 Information Organization

- Place search and discovery features prominently.
- Group property information into overview, features, location, and seller information.
- Group related filters together.
- Present AI results with clear explanations.
- Organize seller dashboard statistics separately from listing management.
- Present subscription plans in a consistent comparison layout.

## 5.13 User-Centered Design

- Support guest browsing before requiring registration.
- Provide guidance for unfamiliar workflows.
- Use plain language instead of unnecessary technical terminology.
- Offer sensible defaults without preventing users from changing them.
- Provide useful empty states and recovery options.
- Avoid unnecessary form fields.
- Ensure the design supports the needs of buyers, sellers, and agents.

